<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Fixes</a> / Laptop charger wattage</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Power compatibility</p>
<h1 class="page-title">How many watts does my laptop charger need? Start with the exact laptop, not a generic chart.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">The safest baseline is the manufacturer’s power specification for the exact laptop model or the rating on the original supported adapter. “65W laptop,” “gaming laptop,” and “USB-C laptop” are not precise enough to select a replacement charger.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>What to check</strong><p>Find the exact laptop model. Check its official manual/specification or original adapter for the supported input and connector. For USB-C charging, confirm which laptop port accepts USB Power Delivery and what wattage/profiles the manufacturer supports. Use a USB-C cable rated for the required current/power. For barrel connectors, match the manufacturer-approved voltage, polarity, plug, and current/wattage requirements—not wattage alone.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Read the laptop manufacturer’s input requirement.</h2>
<p>Search the exact model or service tag on the manufacturer support site. Look for the AC adapter specification, USB-C Power Delivery limit, and whether different hardware configurations ship with different adapters. A discrete-GPU configuration can have a different full-load requirement from a low-power configuration in the same product family.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">USB-C</p><h2>USB-C charging is negotiated end to end.</h2>
<p>A USB-C connector does not guarantee that the port accepts charging, and a charger’s large wattage number does not guarantee that the exact port can deliver that wattage to your laptop. The source, cable, laptop port, and laptop must negotiate a compatible USB Power Delivery profile.</p>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>Confirm the laptop port is documented for USB-C charging/Power Delivery.</li>
<li>Check the charger’s output table for the exact USB-C port you will use.</li>
<li>Use a cable whose documented power rating covers the required current.</li>
<li>On multi-port chargers, check the output allocation when other ports are active.</li>
</ul></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Barrel / proprietary connectors</p><h2>Matching watts is not enough.</h2>
<p>For non-USB-C adapters, use the laptop maker’s approved voltage, polarity, connector, identification protocol where applicable, and current capacity. A physically fitting plug with the wrong electrical specification can damage equipment.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Under load</p><h2>A charger can be compatible and still be slower than the original.</h2>
<p>A lower-power supported USB-C charger may work for light use but charge slowly or lose battery percentage during heavy CPU/GPU load. That does not automatically mean the charger is faulty. Compare the source with the laptop’s documented full-power requirement and the mode you are using.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>What should you buy?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>USB-C laptop</strong><p>Choose a reputable USB Power Delivery charger that supports the exact output profile/wattage your laptop requires, plus a cable rated for that power.</p></div>
<div><strong>Proprietary/barrel laptop</strong><p>Use the manufacturer-supported adapter or a replacement explicitly documented as compatible with the exact model.</p></div>
<div><strong>Docked laptop</strong><p>Check the dock’s maximum power delivered to the host, not only the wattage of the charger plugged into the dock.</p></div>
</div></section>

