<section><p class="page-kicker">See also</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong><a href="/guides/usb-c-charger-charges-phone-not-laptop/">Charger charges phone but not laptop</a></strong><p>Voltage, wattage, and cable limits explained.</p></div></div></section>
<PartnerOffers ids={['usbcCharger']} placement="guide-laptop-charger-wattage" />
<section><p class="page-kicker">The numbers</p><h2>Read the charger label, not just the big wattage number.</h2>
<p>Watts are volts multiplied by amps. For example, an adapter marked <code>20V ⎓ 3.25A</code> corresponds to <strong>65W</strong> at that output. It does not prove that the laptop will negotiate that profile, that another USB-C port offers the same output, or that a multi-port charger will maintain it while charging other devices.</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>USB-C laptop with a supported PD input</strong><p>Confirm the manufacturer's accepted power input, the charger's listed voltage/current profiles, the correct port and a cable rated for the current. A larger maximum watt number does not force extra watts into the device.</p></div>
<div><strong>USB-C laptop that says "slow charger"</strong><p>Check the cable, dock power budget and the active charger's output allocation before blaming the laptop battery.</p></div>
<div><strong>Gaming laptop with barrel input</strong><p>Use its exact supported manufacturer adapter and connector. The same total wattage with incorrect voltage or polarity is not safe compatibility.</p></div>
<div><strong>Laptop connected through a dock</strong><p>Distinguish the charger's input wattage from the dock's maximum host power output. Some power is reserved by the dock and attached equipment.</p></div>
</div>
<h2>A repeatable charger-shopping checklist</h2>
<ol class="article-steps">
<li><strong>Identify the exact laptop SKU</strong><span>Some versions of the same model family use different adapters, depending on CPU and graphics configuration.</span></li>
<li><strong>Check where charging is supported</strong><span>Not all USB-C connectors accept power. A port may support data or video without supporting laptop charging.</span></li>
<li><strong>Read every output label</strong><span>Compare required voltage/current profiles and the power available on the specific charger port with other devices connected.</span></li>
<li><strong>Check the cable</strong><span>A charger rated above 60W may require a properly rated electronically marked cable for higher current under USB Power Delivery.</span></li>
<li><strong>Verify under normal use</strong><span>Observe the laptop's charging indication with the supported source and workload. Do not run prolonged stress tests solely to diagnose a questionable adapter.</span></li>
</ol>
<p>If the original adapter still works, use its documented electrical output as a cross-check, but rely on the manufacturer's supported replacement specifications. See <a href="/guides/laptop-says-slow-charger-with-100w-usb-c-charger/">the 100W slow-charger diagnosis</a> and <a href="/guides/laptop-not-charging-through-usb-c-dock/">dock charging failures</a> for more specific scenarios.</p>
<p class="meta">Reference: <a href="https://www.usb.org/usb-charger-pd">USB Implementers Forum, USB Power Delivery</a>. Numerical example is arithmetic, not a test of a particular charger.</p></section>

